.TH INETD 8C 1/27/83
.UC 4
.SH NAME
inetd \- DARPA little protocol server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.in +.5i
.ti -.5i
.B /etc/inetd
\%[\-d]
\%[\-f\ program]
\%[\-o\ options]
\%[\-q\ program]
protocol/service ...
.in -.5i
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fIinetd\fR server implements a number of the so\-called
\*(lqlittle\*(rq protocols in the IP/TCP protocol suite.
In particular, the following protocols are implemented at this time:
.PP
.nf
.ta \w'Service        'u +\w'RFC  'u
\fBService	RFC  Description\fP
echo	862	sends back whatever you send it
sink	863	throws away whatever you send it
daytime	867	provides the day and time in ASCII
time	868	provides the number of seconds from a reference time
users	866	lists the currently active users
chargen	864	sends you ASCII data
qotd	865	sends you a short ASCII message
finger	742	provides information on the activity of a user
.fi
.PP
See \fIservices\fR\0(5) for the list of ports that \fIinetd\fR will operate at.
The \fIinetd\fR server supports these services using both TCP and UDP
(see \fIprotocols\fR\0(5)).
.PP
If the `\-d' option is specified,
each socket created by \fIinetd\fR will have debugging enabled
(see SO_DEBUG in \fIsocket\fR\0(2)).
If the `\-f' option is given,
the following argument is taken to be the pathname of the program to
run when servicing \fIfinger\fR requests.
Similarly, if the `\-o' option is given,
the following argument is take to be the options that should be given
to this program.
Finally, if the `\-q' option is given,
the following argument is taken to be the pathname of the program to
run when servicing \fIqotd\fR requests.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rfinger(1C)
